 Editors\\
-\emph{Neural Networks}
+\emph{Array}
 
 Dear Editors,
 
-Please consider ``ASCENT: Scale-Complementary External State for Long-Context
-Neural Systems'' as an original full-length Article in the \textbf{Mathematical
-and Computational Analysis} section. This is the closest fit because the
-contribution couples formal information-value and finite-reader interaction
-results with controlled neural-network experiments.
+Please consider ``ASCENT: Scale-Complementary External State for Frozen
+Long-Context Language Models'' as an original regular paper transferred through
+Elsevier's manuscript-transfer service. The work falls directly within
+\emph{Array}'s Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning and Data, Knowledge
+and Intelligent Systems scope: it introduces an external-state interface for
+frozen language models, derives its scale-interaction criterion, and tests that
+criterion through controlled long-context experiments.
 
-\textbf{Contribution to neural networks.}
+This contribution addresses an active \emph{Array} readership: the journal has
+recently published retrieval-augmented optimization for large-model reasoning
+(Wang et al., 2025, doi:10.1016/j.array.2025.100504). ASCENT advances that
+computer-science direction from an application-specific retrieval pipeline to
+a formal, scale-indexed external-state interface evaluated across frozen model
+sizes and families.
+
+\textbf{Contribution.}
 The manuscript formulates scale complementarity: external state complements
 neural reader capacity when its absolute gain increases as the frozen reader
 and a nested state budget are jointly scaled. It connects an
@@ -40,7 +49,7 @@ information-theoretic criterion for nested refinement to an identifiable
 finite-reader interaction and realizes that criterion in ASCENT, a
 target-blind causal writer with bounded query-conditioned readout.
 
-\textbf{Significance.}
+\textbf{Significance and evidence.}
 External-memory evaluations typically ask whether memory helps one model.
 ASCENT instead tests how memory utility changes across scale. In a frozen
 $3\times3$ Qwen2.5 factorial, co-scaled negative-log-likelihood gains rise
@@ -49,30 +58,21 @@ every interaction and gain contrast. Official BABILong evaluations produce
 10.50, 78.88, and 82.63 point gains at 0.5B, 1.5B, and
 3B; five public 7B--8B readers gain 66.75--85.63 points. Cross-node
 reproduction, representation controls, retrieval baselines, and systems
-accounting establish the result as a reproducible neural scaling property.
+accounting establish the result as a reproducible language-model scaling
+property. A third physical-node audit reproduces all Qwen3-8B predictions and
+scientific fields exactly across the complete 800-example official panel.
 
-\textbf{Three most closely related published papers.}
-\begin{enumerate}
-\item Y. Wu et al., ``Memorizing Transformers,'' \emph{ICLR}, 2022,
-establishes transformer external memory; ASCENT identifies and tests how its
-value changes with reader scale.
-\item Y. Kuratov et al., ``BABILong: Testing the Limits of LLMs with Long
-Context Reasoning-in-a-Haystack,'' \emph{NeurIPS Datasets and Benchmarks},
-2024, supplies the official generative tasks; ASCENT adds the interaction
-theory, interface, and factorial evidence.
-\item A. Sorokin et al., ``Q-RAG: Long Context Multi-Step Retrieval via
-Value-Based Embedder Training,'' \emph{ICLR}, 2026, is the closest trained
-retrieval system; frozen ASCENT exceeds its released checkpoints at the two
-larger shared scales without foundation-model training.
-\end{enumerate}
+The manuscript states the exact estimand and tested distribution, and links
+theory, algorithm, and experiment within the main paper. The
+separate Supplementary Material archive contains the core implementation,
+frozen configurations, evaluated panels, scored outputs, model revisions and
+hashes, and executable integrity checks; third-party model weights are not
+redistributed.
 
